					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200743" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1559399955 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Jun 2019 14:39</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4268258">
									

<p>I love the expansion on the Department of Spectral Phenomena, and ghost-busting in general, but the ties to gender dysphoria and such read fairly heavy-handedly to me. The parallel between being forced to stare at your own rotting corpse and being forced to see yourself in a body you don't identify with is a decently clever one (as far as I know; I'm not transgender, so I can't speak for how accurate it is), but would be much more clever if the interview didn't rub it in your face so much.</p>
<p>Ah well. The ending was wholesome enough, and I always enjoy Ethics Committee interactions, so&#8230; yeah. Tentative upvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
